1
00:00:02,212 --> 00:00:03,093
So now that we

2
00:00:03,622 --> 00:00:06,540
know what Deno is, what we can build with

3
00:00:06,588 --> 00:00:09,024
it, and that we installed it, let me give

4
00:00:09,073 --> 00:00:10,852
you an overview over the Deno

5
00:00:11,637 --> 00:00:12,984
ecosystem and

6
00:00:13,561 --> 00:00:16,623
where we'll find the different features we

7
00:00:16,623 --> 00:00:17,873
will use in our code.

8
00:00:18,707 --> 00:00:20,999
And there we got 3 main pillars.

9
00:00:21,592 --> 00:00:24,718
We got the so-called Deno namespace and its

10
00:00:24,862 --> 00:00:28,052
APIs. Basically, that means all the features

11
00:00:28,325 --> 00:00:30,954
that are already built into the Deno tool

12
00:00:31,290 --> 00:00:31,819
you installed.

13
00:00:32,815 --> 00:00:34,822
Then we got something which is called the

14
00:00:34,822 --> 00:00:36,235
standard library.

15
00:00:36,813 --> 00:00:39,783
This is a set of packages maintained by the

16
00:00:39,783 --> 00:00:43,476
Deno team but not built into the Deno tool

17
00:00:43,476 --> 00:00:43,973
itself.

18
00:00:45,242 --> 00:00:46,911
And we got third-party libraries.

19
00:00:47,489 --> 00:00:50,668
Like with any technology, of course, anyone

20
00:00:50,797 --> 00:00:54,217
can build a library for Deno that exposes

21
00:00:54,393 --> 00:00:56,737
certain functionalities which others can use

22
00:00:56,737 --> 00:00:57,797
in their programs.

23
00:00:58,407 --> 00:01:00,317
Now, what are the differences between these

24
00:01:00,574 --> 00:01:03,769
3 pillars and which ones should we use?

25
00:01:04,363 --> 00:01:07,878
Well, the Deno namespace APIs are considered

26
00:01:07,878 --> 00:01:11,009
to be stable and they are maintained by the

27
00:01:11,009 --> 00:01:11,442
core team.

28
00:01:12,133 --> 00:01:15,423
What does stable mean? It means that the

29
00:01:15,504 --> 00:01:18,827
APIs are basically finished. Sure, there

30
00:01:18,987 --> 00:01:20,898
still might be some bugs which need to be

31
00:01:20,898 --> 00:01:23,209
fixed, but we shouldn't expect bigger

32
00:01:23,209 --> 00:01:26,725
changes on those APIs. If we write some code

33
00:01:26,757 --> 00:01:29,535
depending on those APIs today, it should

34
00:01:29,551 --> 00:01:31,285
still work in a year from now,

35
00:01:31,879 --> 00:01:34,047
even if we then update to the latest version

36
00:01:34,047 --> 00:01:34,898
of Deno by then.

37
00:01:35,974 --> 00:01:37,884
That's different for the standard library.

38
00:01:38,478 --> 00:01:41,128
The standard library is also maintained by

39
00:01:41,128 --> 00:01:44,387
the core team, but it's currently unstable.

40
00:01:45,142 --> 00:01:47,036
Now, by the point of time you're watching

41
00:01:47,069 --> 00:01:49,509
this, this might have changed. I'll show you

42
00:01:49,798 --> 00:01:50,697
where you can check it,

43
00:01:51,227 --> 00:01:53,620
but it will definitely be unstable for quite

44
00:01:53,620 --> 00:01:57,068
quite a while and unstable simply means

45
00:01:57,822 --> 00:02:01,431
that there might be more bugs and that we

46
00:02:01,431 --> 00:02:04,511
might see changes to features coming from

47
00:02:04,559 --> 00:02:05,169
that library

48
00:02:05,826 --> 00:02:07,254
that might break our code.

49
00:02:07,831 --> 00:02:10,879
So some parts of that library might be

50
00:02:10,975 --> 00:02:13,943
updated such that we also need to update our

51
00:02:13,943 --> 00:02:16,718
code to keep it working. So there might be

52
00:02:16,750 --> 00:02:17,889
breaking changes

53
00:02:18,659 --> 00:02:21,642
and well, that's never great. As a

54
00:02:21,642 --> 00:02:23,987
developer, but of course, the advantage or

55
00:02:24,051 --> 00:02:25,817
the good thing is that it means that the

56
00:02:25,817 --> 00:02:28,242
Deno team is working on it, but we just have

57
00:02:28,242 --> 00:02:30,169
to be aware of it and then we have to deal

58
00:02:30,217 --> 00:02:30,490
with it.

59
00:02:31,582 --> 00:02:34,135
And then we got third-party libraries. These

60
00:02:34,327 --> 00:02:36,832
are not managed by the Deno core team.

61
00:02:36,977 --> 00:02:39,433
Instead, they are managed by community teams

62
00:02:39,867 --> 00:02:42,195
and therefore their stability differs. It

63
00:02:42,195 --> 00:02:44,652
really depends on the individual library.

64
00:02:45,230 --> 00:02:46,787
Now, third-party libraries could be

65
00:02:46,787 --> 00:02:49,999
something like a library that helps you work

66
00:02:50,063 --> 00:02:53,208
with MongoDB, which is a database, for

67
00:02:53,256 --> 00:02:55,246
example. It's just an example.

68
00:02:55,904 --> 00:02:58,167
Such a library could expose certain

69
00:02:58,167 --> 00:03:00,173
functionalities that make working with a

70
00:03:00,237 --> 00:03:02,869
database easier, and that's of course great

71
00:03:02,901 --> 00:03:05,196
and useful, and therefore we might be using

72
00:03:05,228 --> 00:03:05,918
such a library.

73
00:03:06,673 --> 00:03:09,256
But as I said, you then really have to check

74
00:03:09,305 --> 00:03:11,246
the different libraries to find out whether

75
00:03:11,246 --> 00:03:12,306
they're stable or not.

76
00:03:13,156 --> 00:03:15,467
And the maintenance thing of course means

77
00:03:15,579 --> 00:03:19,128
that Whilst for the core team, you can rely

78
00:03:19,497 --> 00:03:21,441
on them to keep on working on Deno,

79
00:03:22,067 --> 00:03:24,701
for third-party libraries, you can't rely on

80
00:03:24,813 --> 00:03:25,022
that.

81
00:03:25,857 --> 00:03:27,495
A team or an individual

82
00:03:28,025 --> 00:03:30,964
that released a library that, for example,

83
00:03:30,980 --> 00:03:32,939
makes working with MongoDB easier

84
00:03:33,549 --> 00:03:36,039
might stop working on that in the future,

85
00:03:36,520 --> 00:03:39,266
and then you might have a library that's not

86
00:03:39,266 --> 00:03:40,342
being updated anymore.

87
00:03:40,953 --> 00:03:43,698
This could potentially be a problem And it

88
00:03:43,698 --> 00:03:45,686
simply means that these are things you

89
00:03:45,686 --> 00:03:48,155
should have on your radar so that you're

90
00:03:48,235 --> 00:03:50,447
able to react if something is not working

91
00:03:50,495 --> 00:03:51,505
there the way it should.

92
00:03:52,820 --> 00:03:54,086
Now, that's the stability and the

93
00:03:54,086 --> 00:03:54,632
maintenance.

94
00:03:55,209 --> 00:03:57,437
What are the core differences between these

95
00:03:57,710 --> 00:04:00,980
3 pillars though? Why isn't everything built

96
00:04:00,980 --> 00:04:01,557
into Deno?

97
00:04:02,262 --> 00:04:04,539
Well, the idea of Deno, of course, is to be

98
00:04:04,587 --> 00:04:05,036
a small

99
00:04:05,709 --> 00:04:09,396
handy tool which is not packed with stuff

100
00:04:09,396 --> 00:04:10,390
you might never need.

101
00:04:11,079 --> 00:04:11,432
Therefore,

102
00:04:11,977 --> 00:04:15,632
the Deno namespace APIs are the only APIs,

103
00:04:15,664 --> 00:04:18,133
the only features that are built into the

104
00:04:18,133 --> 00:04:21,627
Deno tool itself. And built into Deno simply

105
00:04:21,675 --> 00:04:24,112
means that we don't have to install or

106
00:04:24,304 --> 00:04:27,270
import these features. They're just

107
00:04:27,302 --> 00:04:30,540
available on a global Deno object without

108
00:04:30,797 --> 00:04:32,913
any extra work being required.

109
00:04:34,179 --> 00:04:36,231
Now, the standard library is a bit

110
00:04:36,263 --> 00:04:39,309
different. There we do have to add extra

111
00:04:39,389 --> 00:04:42,002
imports to our scripts in order to use

112
00:04:42,098 --> 00:04:44,727
certain features from a standard library

113
00:04:44,759 --> 00:04:45,288
module.

114
00:04:45,945 --> 00:04:48,414
And I'm using the terms module and package

115
00:04:48,670 --> 00:04:49,648
interchangeably here.

116
00:04:50,914 --> 00:04:53,591
The third-party libraries, well, we also

117
00:04:53,639 --> 00:04:55,739
have to import those in order to use

118
00:04:55,739 --> 00:04:58,048
features. So that's the difference. Imports

119
00:04:58,048 --> 00:05:00,949
required for standard and third-party, not

120
00:05:00,949 --> 00:05:03,242
required for the Deno namespace APIs.

121
00:05:04,075 --> 00:05:06,560
But as I said, the Deno namespace APIs are

122
00:05:06,560 --> 00:05:09,301
focused on being very slim and small and

123
00:05:09,589 --> 00:05:12,860
fast. And therefore here, we really only got

124
00:05:12,876 --> 00:05:12,892
a

125
00:05:13,437 --> 00:05:17,113
small set of low-level core functionalities,

126
00:05:17,466 --> 00:05:19,263
for example, for writing a file.

127
00:05:20,098 --> 00:05:22,970
Theoretically, with these namespace APIs,

128
00:05:23,083 --> 00:05:24,880
you got everything you need to build

129
00:05:24,928 --> 00:05:25,827
whatever you want,

130
00:05:26,373 --> 00:05:29,021
but it would mean that you have to reinvent

131
00:05:29,021 --> 00:05:31,412
the wheel a lot. If you want to spin up a

132
00:05:31,412 --> 00:05:33,883
web server, you have to do all the listening

133
00:05:34,060 --> 00:05:37,254
and package parsing and all the nitty-gritty

134
00:05:37,350 --> 00:05:39,854
details that are involved with spinning up a

135
00:05:39,854 --> 00:05:41,349
web server on your own.

136
00:05:42,104 --> 00:05:44,755
You might be willing to do that, but most

137
00:05:44,755 --> 00:05:46,860
people will not be willing to do that, and

138
00:05:46,860 --> 00:05:49,142
it might even be insecure if you tried it on

139
00:05:49,174 --> 00:05:50,653
your own and you don't know what you're

140
00:05:50,701 --> 00:05:50,990
doing.

141
00:05:52,067 --> 00:05:54,943
Therefore, the standard library builds up on

142
00:05:54,943 --> 00:05:57,835
these low-level core functionalities and

143
00:05:57,916 --> 00:06:00,953
gives you utility functionalities that, for

144
00:06:00,985 --> 00:06:03,363
example, make spinning up a web server much

145
00:06:03,540 --> 00:06:04,729
easier and more convenient.

146
00:06:05,484 --> 00:06:07,699
And it's the same for third-party libraries.

147
00:06:08,213 --> 00:06:11,119
Those also build up on those core low-level

148
00:06:11,168 --> 00:06:13,897
functionalities and on the standard library.

149
00:06:14,154 --> 00:06:15,744
They also might build up on that,

150
00:06:16,386 --> 00:06:19,180
and then they give you even nicer access to

151
00:06:19,212 --> 00:06:21,299
certain features, or they provide

152
00:06:21,299 --> 00:06:23,660
functionalities which neither the core

153
00:06:23,708 --> 00:06:26,566
namespace APIs nor the standard library

154
00:06:26,903 --> 00:06:29,215
provides like this, for example, working

155
00:06:29,231 --> 00:06:30,066
with MongoDB.

156
00:06:31,477 --> 00:06:34,732
That is why we have these 3 pillars and why

157
00:06:34,764 --> 00:06:37,825
we will use modules from each pillar. We'll

158
00:06:37,825 --> 00:06:40,118
not use just the namespace APIs, for

159
00:06:40,150 --> 00:06:42,474
example, because that would mean that we

160
00:06:42,474 --> 00:06:45,889
have to reinvent the wheel a lot. Maybe we

161
00:06:45,921 --> 00:06:48,550
would even introduce horrible bugs and we

162
00:06:48,550 --> 00:06:50,922
would spend a lot of our time on doing

163
00:06:50,970 --> 00:06:53,535
something that was already done by other

164
00:06:53,535 --> 00:06:56,084
people. So by using the standard library and

165
00:06:56,180 --> 00:06:58,344
other third-party libraries, we as a

166
00:06:58,344 --> 00:07:00,893
developer can focus on our core business

167
00:07:00,909 --> 00:07:03,325
logic and we don't have to reinvent the

168
00:07:03,325 --> 00:07:04,670
wheel over and over again.
